5C. The Module Renderer
The Module Renderer is a single Flutter widget — ModuleCard — that takes a ModuleSpec and a Surface and renders the appropriate UI. It uses a switch on ModuleSpec.type to delegate to a type-specific sub-widget. This is the one widget you write per new type:

class ModuleCard extends StatelessWidget {
  final ModuleSpec spec;
  final Surface surface;
  final dynamic currentValue; // null for live modules
  final Function(dynamic)? onValueChanged; // null for live modules

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return switch (spec.type) {
      ModuleType.countdown => CountdownCard(spec: spec),
      ModuleType.countup => CountupCard(spec: spec),
      ModuleType.checkbox => CheckboxCard(spec: spec, value: currentValue, onChange: onValueChanged),
      ModuleType.rating => RatingCard(spec: spec, value: currentValue, onChange: onValueChanged),
      ModuleType.data_input => DataInputCard(spec: spec, value: currentValue, onChange: onValueChanged),
      ModuleType.progress_bar => ProgressBarCard(spec: spec),
      ModuleType.streak_counter => StreakCard(spec: spec),
      ModuleType.text_note => TextNoteCard(spec: spec, value: currentValue, onChange: onValueChanged),
      ModuleType.mandatory_session => MandatorySessionCard(spec: spec, value: currentValue),
    };
  }
}

Every new module type you add in future requires: one enum constant, one case in this switch, one sub-widget. That is the entire cost of extensibility.

 
PART 6 — Database Schema for the Module Engine

6A. New and Updated Tables
The v3 schema introduced DayPlan, WeekPlan, and ModuleInstance. v4 extends and clarifies these to accommodate live modules, homescreen placement, and the generic config system.

ModuleSpecs table (Drift / SQLite)
One row per user-defined module. This is the master library.

class ModuleSpecsTable extends Table {
  TextColumn get id => text()();                        // PK: 'holiday_countdown'
  TextColumn get typeStr => text()();                   // 'countdown', 'checkbox', etc.
  TextColumn get label => text()();                     // 'Holiday Countdown'
  TextColumn get emoji => text().nullable()();          // '🏖'
  TextColumn get configJson => text()();               // JSON blob
  TextColumn get placementsJson => text()();           // JSON array of surface strings
  BoolColumn get isLive => boolean().withDefault(const Constant(false))();
  BoolColumn get required => boolean().withDefault(const Constant(false))();
  BoolColumn get showInSummary => boolean().withDefault(const Constant(true))();
  BoolColumn get isArchived => boolean().withDefault(const Constant(false))();
  DateTimeColumn get createdAt => dateTime()();
  @override Set<Column> get primaryKey => {id};
}

ModuleValues table (Drift / SQLite)
One row per logged value. Live modules have no rows here. Logged modules get one row per surface instance per day (or per session for session-scoped modules).

class ModuleValuesTable extends Table {
  IntColumn get id => integer().autoIncrement()();
  TextColumn get moduleSpecId => text()();              // FK to ModuleSpecsTable
  TextColumn get surfaceStr => text()();               // 'day', 'session', etc.
  TextColumn get contextKey => text()();               // date string OR session ID
  TextColumn get valueJson => text().nullable()();     // JSON: bool, int, float, string
  DateTimeColumn get loggedAt => dateTime()();
  DateTimeColumn get updatedAt => dateTime()();
}

contextKey is either a date string ('2026-02-22') for day-scoped modules or a session UUID for session-scoped modules. This single table handles all logged module types across all surfaces.

HomescreenLayout table (Drift / SQLite)
Stores which modules are pinned to each zone of the Homescreen and in what order.

class HomescreenLayoutTable extends Table {
  IntColumn get id => integer().autoIncrement()();
  TextColumn get moduleSpecId => text()();
  IntColumn get zone => integer()();                    // 1 = Live, 3 = Logged
  IntColumn get sortOrder => integer()();
}

Firestore mirror structure
Firestore mirrors the local SQLite schema for sync. The collection paths:

/users/{uid}/moduleSpecs/{specId}          ← mirrors ModuleSpecsTable
/users/{uid}/moduleValues/{contextKey}     ← map of specId → valueJson for that day
/users/{uid}/homescreenLayout              ← single document with zone arrays

6B. The Live Module Runtime
Live modules are never stored in ModuleValues — their values are computed on every render from their config. This means:
•	Countdown: value = config.targetDate.difference(DateTime.now())
•	Countup: value = DateTime.now().difference(config.originDate)
•	Progress bar: value = (DateTime.now() - config.startDate) / (config.endDate - config.startDate)
•	Streak counter: value = query ModuleValues for sourceModuleId, count consecutive days with a truthy value ending at yesterday
The streak counter is the only live module that requires a database read — the others are pure arithmetic. This query is cached and invalidated at midnight.

 
PART 7 — Creative Additions

7A. The 'Day X of Y' Aesthetic
One small but powerful detail: everywhere in the app that shows a date from an active plan, FlowState should also show the plan day number. Not just 'Tuesday, February 22' but 'Tuesday, February 22 · Day 1 of 136'. This creates a sense of being inside a narrative, not just tracking data. The Homescreen Today Snapshot, the Day Dashboard header, the notification, the Now Bar — all of them should surface this when a plan is active.
The implementation is trivial: it's a computed field on DayPlan. But psychologically it transforms the app from a tool into a journey.

7B. Module Groups
When a user has many modules, the Homescreen and Day Dashboard can become cluttered. Module Groups solve this without adding a new data structure: a group is just a ModuleSpec with type = group that contains an ordered list of child module IDs. The group renders as a collapsed card with a label and a summary line ('3 of 4 done'), expandable with a tap. This lets a user group related modules: 'Morning Routine' (skincare AM, vitamins, water), 'Evening Routine' (skincare PM, journalled), 'Health Stats' (steps, weight).

7C. The 'Intention' Field on Countdown Timers
When a user creates a countdown, they are prompted for one optional field: 'What does this date mean to you?' (max 140 characters). This is stored as config.intention. It surfaces on the Countdown Detail Screen as a small italic line beneath the date label: 'To prove to yourself that discipline compounds.'
This sounds minor. It is not. It is the difference between a number and a reason. Every time the user opens that screen, they see why the countdown exists. It is particularly powerful for long-range goals where motivation dips mid-way.

7D. The 'Last Seen' Countup
A special countup variant where the counter resets automatically when the user logs a specific activity. Config: { 'resetOnModuleId': 'workout_done', 'label': 'Days since last workout' }. When the checkbox module 'workout_done' is ticked, this countup resets to 0 and starts climbing again. This creates a 'how long has it been?' accountability display without any user management. A fitness tracker equivalent of the 'days since last accident' sign.

7E. The Homescreen as a Widget
On Android, the Homescreen module grid can be exposed as a home screen widget (the Android launcher widget, distinct from the FlowState Homescreen). A 4x2 widget showing: the active countdown, today's title and day number, and a row of module completion dots. One tap opens FlowState directly to the Today view. This is the most glanceable possible version of FlowState — you see your mission and your progress without unlocking your phone.

7F. Quiet Days
A Quiet Day is a DayPlan with no sessions, no required modules, and a special status flag: 'quiet'. It appears in the Week view as a different colour (muted, not grey which implies missed). The user can import quiet days via CSV ('quiet' in a special column) or mark any day as quiet manually. The weekly narrative acknowledges quiet days differently: 'You took Saturday off. Intentional rest is part of the plan.' This matters because apps that treat every missed day as a failure create a punitive relationship with planning. Quiet days make rest a first-class concept in the system.

7G. The Streak Display Format Idea
For countdown timers that are close to a milestone (e.g. 3 days until a 100-day streak, 7 days until an exam), the Homescreen card subtly changes its visual style — the border gets an animated gradient, the number gets slightly larger. It is not a pop-up, not a notification, not a badge. Just a visual intensity shift that communicates 'you're getting close' without demanding attention. The threshold for this change is configurable per-module (defaulting to 10% of the total range or 7 days, whichever is smaller).

 
